# Frontend

## Rules

No business logic. If the UI needs a rule, the API already enforces it — call it.

Permission checks (`can()`) decide what to render. They are a convenience, never
a control.

Data through TanStack Query. Never retry a 4xx: a 401 or 403 is an answer, not a
transient failure.

Clear the query cache on sign-in and sign-out, or the next person to use the
browser sees the previous user's identity and data.

## Every screen needs

Loading state, empty state, error state. §61: a feature is not done because the
happy path renders.

An entitlement error (402) shows a plan message, not a permission error.

## Accessibility (WCAG 2.2 AA)

- Semantic HTML: real `button`, real `label`, real `table`.
- Never remove the focus ring. Restyle it if you must.
- Status is never colour alone — every status badge carries text and a glyph, so
  it survives greyscale printing and colour blindness.
- Wide tables scroll inside their own container, never the page.
- A skip link, and a visible `aria-current` on the active nav item.

## Design

Institutional and dense: this is software people look at for eight hours while
deciding who may operate a forklift. Use the tokens in
`packages/ui/src/styles.css`; do not introduce ad-hoc colours.
